{#
  JSON-LD graph. One <script> per page, included by the base layout.

  ============================================================
  EVERY VALUE HERE IS READ, NEVER TYPED.
  ============================================================

  The NAP comes from site.nap, which is the canonical object the footer
  colophon and the Contact page already render. That is not a convention, it
  is the whole point: CLAUDE.md requires schema to match visible content
  exactly, and a NAP that disagrees with itself across a site is an
  entity-resolution problem. Because both the visible pane and this block
  read the same keys, they cannot drift. site.nap's keys were deliberately
  named for schema.org PostalAddress, so they map across with no transcription
  step. See SCHEMA.md.

  The visible email is obfuscated (no contiguous address, no mailto, assembled
  in main.js). This block carries the real one. That is correct and not a
  contradiction: the obfuscation defends against naive scrapers reading raw
  HTML, while schema exists to be machine-read by engines that should have it.

  ONE GRAPH, NOT SEVERAL BLOCKS. Every page emits the Organization and WebSite
  nodes under stable @ids and then references them by @id from the page-level
  nodes, so an engine crawling five pages reconciles one organization rather
  than five near-duplicates.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and why. Do not "fill these in" without the
  underlying fact:

    sameAs        No verified external profile exists anywhere in this repo.
                  Not GBP, not Yelp, not social. An unverified profile URL is
                  a claim about identity, and a wrong one splits the entity
                  instead of consolidating it. Add each ONLY once confirmed.
    aggregateRating / Review
                  There is no sourced review data. The two testimonials on the
                  site are real and attributed but carry no rating and no
                  source, and self-collected reviews about your own business
                  are ineligible for review rich results regardless.
    LocalBusiness The type is Organization, NOT LocalBusiness. LocalBusiness
                  asserts a place customers visit, and the honest supporting
                  facts are missing: no opening hours, no geo coordinates, no
                  verified Google Business Profile, and a "#267" suite line
                  that reads like a mailbox. Upgrading the @type is a one-line
                  change once a verified GBP, hours and geo exist; asserting
                  it now would be inventing a storefront.
    Results cases The Results page's case studies are still bracketed
                  placeholders ("[+XX%]", "[Approach and outcome narrative to
                  be supplied]"). Encoding those would publish fabricated
                  outcomes. That page gets its page-level nodes only, and the
                  ItemList lands when the real numbers do.
#}

{#- The organization. Referenced by @id from every other node, on every page. -#}
{% set organization = {
  "@type": "Organization",
  "@id": site.url + "/#organization",
  "name": site.nap.name,
  "url": site.url + "/",
  "description": site.description,
  "logo": {
    "@type": "ImageObject",
    "@id": site.url + "/#logo",
    "url": site.url + site.logo,
    "caption": site.nap.name
  },
  "image": { "@id": site.url + "/#logo" },
  "address": {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    "streetAddress": site.nap.streetAddress,
    "addressLocality": site.nap.addressLocality,
    "addressRegion": site.nap.addressRegion,
    "postalCode": site.nap.postalCode,
    "addressCountry": site.nap.addressCountry
  },
  "telephone": site.nap.telephone,
  "email": site.nap.email,
  "foundingDate": site.founded,
  "foundingLocation": {
    "@type": "Place",
    "address": {
      "@type": "PostalAddress",
      "addressLocality": site.nap.addressLocality,
      "addressRegion": site.nap.addressRegion,
      "addressCountry": site.nap.addressCountry
    }
  }
} %}

{% set website = {
  "@type": "WebSite",
  "@id": site.url + "/#website",
  "url": site.url + "/",
  "name": site.name,
  "description": site.description,
  "publisher": { "@id": site.url + "/#organization" },
  "inLanguage": "en"
} %}

{#- Through the absoluteUrl filter, which is also what base.njk emits as the
    canonical. Same definition, one place, so the URL this graph hangs off and
    the URL the page declares itself to be cannot drift apart. -#}
{%- set pageUrl = page.url | absoluteUrl -%}

{#- Breadcrumb label for this page, LOOKED UP from nav.js rather than restated.
    The nav already holds the short human label for each of these URLs; the
    front-matter title is a long keyword-aligned string and reads badly in a
    breadcrumb. Home is not in nav.primary, so it falls back to the site name
    and the trail is a single crumb. -#}
{#- Collected into an array rather than assigned to a string, because a plain
    `set` inside a Nunjucks `for` is scoped to the loop and does not survive
    it. Mutating an array declared outside does. Same idiom as `graph` below. -#}
{% set crumbMatch = [] %}
{% for item in nav.primary %}
  {% if item.url == page.url %}{% set crumbMatch = (crumbMatch.push(item.label), crumbMatch) %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% set crumbLabel = crumbMatch[0] %}

{% set crumbs = [{
  "@type": "ListItem",
  "position": 1,
  "name": site.name,
  "item": site.url + "/"
}] %}
{% if crumbLabel %}
  {% set crumbs = (crumbs.push({
    "@type": "ListItem",
    "position": 2,
    "name": crumbLabel,
    "item": pageUrl
  }), crumbs) %}
{% endif %}

{% set breadcrumb = {
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "@id": pageUrl + "#breadcrumb",
  "itemListElement": crumbs
} %}

{#- schemaType comes from each page's front matter (AboutPage, ContactPage,
    CollectionPage), defaulting to WebPage. Kept in front matter rather than
    derived from the URL here, so a page declares its own kind where the rest
    of its content lives. -#}
{% set webpage = {
  "@type": schemaType or "WebPage",
  "@id": pageUrl + "#webpage",
  "url": pageUrl,
  "name": title or site.title,
  "description": description or site.description,
  "isPartOf": { "@id": site.url + "/#website" },
  "about": { "@id": site.url + "/#organization" },
  "breadcrumb": { "@id": pageUrl + "#breadcrumb" },
  "inLanguage": "en"
} %}

{% set graph = [organization, website, webpage, breadcrumb] %}

{#- Team. Present only on Who We Are, where `team` is in front matter and the
    same objects render the visible cards, so name and jobTitle match what a
    reader sees character for character.

    jobTitle carries `role` verbatim, including the " · English / Spanish"
    suffix three members have. That suffix is a real client-facing fact stated
    on the visible card, and splitting it into knowsLanguage would mean
    parsing display copy and emitting a value no reader can see. Verbatim
    keeps schema and page identical, which is the rule that matters. -#}
{% if team %}
  {% for member in team %}
    {% set graph = (graph.push({
      "@type": "Person",
      "@id": pageUrl + "#person-" + loop.index,
      "name": member.name,
      "jobTitle": member.role,
      "description": member.bio,
      "worksFor": { "@id": site.url + "/#organization" }
    }), graph) %}
  {% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{#- Services. Present only on What We Do, flattened out of the three outcome
    groups; each service keeps its group in `category` so the page's own
    structure survives without three levels of nested OfferCatalog. Names and
    descriptions are the same front-matter strings the page renders. -#}
{% if outcomes %}
  {% set serviceItems = [] %}
  {% for outcome in outcomes %}
    {% for service in outcome.services %}
      {% set serviceItems = (serviceItems.push({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        "position": serviceItems.length + 1,
        "item": {
          "@type": "Service",
          "name": service.name,
          "description": service.desc,
          "category": outcome.title,
          "provider": { "@id": site.url + "/#organization" }
        }
      }), serviceItems) %}
    {% endfor %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% set graph = (graph.push({
    "@type": "ItemList",
    "@id": pageUrl + "#services",
    "name": title,
    "itemListElement": serviceItems
  }), graph) %}
{% endif %}

<script type="application/ld+json">
{{ { "@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": graph } | jsonld | safe }}
</script>
